{
  "dom_id": "question-167517",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "Determine which of $x=6$, $x=9,$ and $x=10$ is a root of $p(x),$ and use this information to write down a factor of $p(x)$ in the form $(x-r).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        is a factor of $p(x)$",
  "worked_solution": "The factor theorem states that $(x-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we consider the numbers $6$, $9,$ and $10$ as possible roots. Let's check each of these values in turn.\n\n- $x=6$ is not a root of $p(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =(6)^{3}+3(6)^{2}-972 \\\\  & =216+108-972 \\\\  & =-648 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $x=9$ is a root of $p(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(9) & =(9)^{3}+3(9)^{2}-972 \\\\  & =729+243-972 \\\\  & =972-972 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $x=10$ is not a root of $p(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(10) & =(10)^{3}+3(10)^{2}-972 \\\\  & =1\\,000+300-972 \\\\  & =328 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the factor theorem, $(x-9)$ is a factor of $p(x).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
